# env.example — the environment variables this project understands.
#
# Nothing in this file is secret: it is a TEMPLATE. Real values go in files
# that git ignores (the repository .gitignore excludes `.env` and `.env.*`):
#
#   cp env.example .env.prod      # then edit .env.prod with the real remote values
#   set -a; source .env.prod; set +a          # load it into the current shell
#   python check_connection.py                # verify before doing anything else
#
# For the LOCAL server you don't need a file at all:
#   eval "$(python local_server.py env)"      # or simply use the make targets
#
# See docs/05_remote_database.md for a step-by-step guide.

# Keep the single quotes: URLs contain characters (& ? #) the shell would misread.

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The one connection string used by the Python code AND by Atlas.
#   postgres://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:PORT/DATABASE?sslmode=MODE
# URL-encode special characters in the password (@ : / # ? %):
#   python -c "from urllib.parse import quote; print(quote('p@ss/word', safe=''))"
DATABASE_URL='postgres://app_user:CHANGE_ME@db.example.com:5432/app?sslmode=require'

# local | prod — with prod, config.py refuses connections without TLS (sslmode)
APP_ENV='prod'

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only needed by Atlas commands that compute SQL (migrate diff / validate / down).
# Always an EMPTY scratch database — normally your local server's atlas_dev:
ATLAS_DEV_URL='postgres://postgres:localdev@127.0.0.1:5433/atlas_dev?sslmode=disable'

# Only for `make rehearse` / `python local_server.py clone`: the database to copy.
# Use a READ-ONLY user.
# SOURCE_URL='postgres://readonly_user:CHANGE_ME@db.example.com:5432/app?sslmode=require'
# CLONE_WITH_DATA='0'

# Alternative to DATABASE_URL (config.py builds the URL from these if it is unset)
# PGHOST='db.example.com'
# PGPORT='5432'
# PGUSER='app_user'
# PGPASSWORD='CHANGE_ME'
# PGDATABASE='app'
# PGSSLMODE='require'
